# Normalise line endings for text, but never touch binaries.
*                text=auto

# OfficeIMO writes PDFs with UNCOMPRESSED content streams, so they contain
# few NUL bytes and git's binary auto-detection guesses "text". It then does
# LF->CRLF on checkout and injects stray \r into the file - result.pdf gained
# 1743 bytes that way, corrupting its xref offsets. These are test fixtures
# and cited evidence, so they must round-trip byte-for-byte.
*.pdf            binary
*.doc            binary
*.docx           binary
*.xlsx           binary
*.ppt            binary
*.pptx           binary
*.png            binary
*.jpg            binary
*.nupkg          binary
*.snupkg         binary

# Real pages from the govdocs1 crawl, kept as regression fixtures and as cited evidence. They are
# TEXT, but `* text=auto` normalises them - two of the four arrived with CRLF from the .gov servers
# that served them in the 2000s and were rewritten to LF on the way into the index. Nothing about
# the defects they exercise depends on that, but assets/realworld/README.txt says they are kept
# whole and must not be edited, and a file git rewrote on commit is not kept whole.
#
# Same reasoning as the PDFs above: fixtures that are evidence must round-trip byte-for-byte.
tests/DocToolkit.Tests/assets/realworld/*.html binary

# Shell scripts must keep LF even when checked out on Windows: `* text=auto`
# above would give them CRLF, and bash on Linux then fails with
# `$'\r': command not found`. scripts/ runs in CI on ubuntu-latest.
*.sh             text eol=lf
